@page "/results"
@inject DemoData Data

<PageTitle>Results</PageTitle>

<h1>Above the baseline, below training</h1>
<p class="lede">
    Every network was scored on the same held-out matches from three tracking sources. spellF1
    counts whole possessions recovered, so both flicker and merging cost.
</p>

<div class="card chart">@((MarkupString)Charts.Results(Data.Benchmark))</div>
<p class="note">For slides: <a href="/charts/results.svg">results.svg</a> · <a href="/charts/results.svg?theme=dark">dark</a></p>

<div class="card tablewrap" style="margin: 1rem 0">
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>spellF1</th>
                @foreach (string s in Benchmark.SourceOrder) { <th>@Data.Benchmark.SourceNames[s]</th> }
                <th>weights set</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var row in Data.Benchmark.Rows)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@row.Label</td>
                    @foreach (string s in Benchmark.SourceOrder) { <td>@row.Sources[s].SpellF1.ToString("0.000")</td> }
                    <td>@(Data.Benchmark.Weights.TryGetValue(row.Label, out long w) ? w.ToString("N0") : "–")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 style="margin-top: 1.6rem">Feedback, pass by pass</h2>
<p>
    Experiment B: after each reply the network was scored on practice matches and the model was told
    how it did. Pass 5 is the one to show: a new idea made things much worse, and the next reply
    names the mechanism of its own bug and fixes it.
</p>
<div class="card chart">@((MarkupString)Charts.Passes(Data.PassScores))</div>
<p class="note">For slides: <a href="/charts/passes.svg">passes.svg</a> · <a href="/charts/passes.svg?theme=dark">dark</a></p>

<div class="grid two" style="margin-top: 1rem">
    <div class="card">
        <h2>What to take from it</h2>
        <p>Where the ball is visible, 179 hand-written weights get close to 698,115 trained ones
            (0.784 against 0.842 on Metrica). Where it is hidden for long stretches they do not
            (0.405 against 0.724 on DFL): what training adds is inference while the ball is unseen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h2>What it is not</h2>
        <p>Not a better model: training wins on every source. One run per experiment, so the spread
            between runs is unknown, and differences under about 0.04 are noise. The full account,
            including what went wrong, is in <code>experiment/REPORT.md</code>.</p>
    </div>
</div>
